# The History of the Netherlands

The Netherlands has a rich and storied history that stretches back over two thousand years, when the region was inhabited by Germanic tribes and later came under Roman influence. During the Middle Ages, these low-lying lands were a patchwork of counties and duchies, gradually reclaimed from the sea through an ingenious system of dikes, canals, and windmills that defined the Dutch relationship with water. The modern nation emerged in 1581 when the seven provinces declared independence from Spanish rule, launching the Eighty Years' War. The following century, known as the **Dutch Golden Age** (roughly 1600–1672), transformed the country into a global powerhouse of trade, art, and science. The establishment of the Dutch East India Company (VOC) in 1602—the world's first joint-stock company—spurred an extensive colonial empire spanning the Americas, Africa, and Asia. Though its global dominance waned in the 18th century, the Netherlands emerged as a prosperous, influential nation in the modern era, renowned for its democratic traditions, innovative agriculture, and enduring contributions to art, science, and global commerce.decode: 354 tokens in 31.8s (11.12 tok/s)
